mbines development with immediate tactical threats, putting maximum pressure on Black's uncastled king.\n\n**Reason:**\nQh5 is the most forcing move. It attacks two critical points simultaneously (f7 and the g5 knight) while Black's king is still in the center. This creates immediate tactical problems that are difficult to solve - Black cannot adequately defend both the f7 pawn and the g5 knight. This aggressive approach capitalizes on White's lead in development and Black's king safety issues.\n\n**Answer:** Q: d1-h5"}
